# Quick Setup Guide

1. Clone and Configure the Script
Download the script file into your current project folder containing your targeted application Dockerfile. Open the script file with a text editor and configure the header setup variables according to your stack requirements:

```bash
# Explicit runtime configuration variables
DOCKER_IMAGE_NAME="your-custom-image"
DOCKER_CONTAINER_NAME="your-custom-container"
DOCKER_REGISTRY_USER="your_dockerhub_account"
DOCKER_SANDBOX_PORT=8080

    Update System Permissions
    To allow shell execution rights inside your Unix environment, assign the executable bits to the asset:

Bash

chmod +x docker-menu.sh

    Execution
    Launch the interactive orchestrator console using your preferred terminal interface:

Bash

./docker-menu.sh

Enterprise Workflow Application Context

This operational tool serves as a secure bridge for Decoupled Architecture Releases. Developers can maintain private text structures on standard versioning spaces (such as private GitHub repositories), while utilizing this workflow automation client to push final, built immutable binaries over Docker Hub registries straight to runtime cloud providers (such as Render, AWS, or GCP).

This workflow pattern insulates master source repositories from infrastructure tokens, minimizing exploit surfaces in multi-tenant environments.
Technical Instructions to Publish Upstream on GitHub

    Access your upstream GitHub account interface and create a clean public target repository named smart-docker-orchestrator or docker-lifecycle-utility.

    Initialize an isolated local directory boundary on your development machine host:

Bash

   mkdir docker-cli-tool && cd docker-cli-tool
   git init

    Commit the structural code bases (docker-menu.sh and README.md) straight into your newly generated repository path layout.

    Establish tracking branch linkages and deploy the assets upstream to your remote cloud space:

Bash

   git add .
   git commit -m "feat: initial release of smart docker management utility"
   git branch -M main
   git remote add origin git@github.com:JuanG206/your-repository-name.git
   git push -u origin main
